# Template for apps/worker/.dev.vars — copy, then fill in real values:
#
#   cp .dev.vars.example .dev.vars
#
# .dev.vars is what `wrangler dev` reads for local secrets. It is gitignored
# (.gitignore:41); this .example file is the only variant committed. In
# production these are set with `wrangler secret put <NAME>` and never live in
# a file. Never paste a real value into this file. See spec §15.8.
#
# The test harness DOES load this file, but vitest.config.ts's explicit miniflare
# bindings take precedence for the keys it sets, so tests run against fixtures
# ("test-signing-secret", "xoxb-test") regardless of what is here. Don't rely on
# a value in this file reaching a test.

# Slack app signing secret, used to verify the X-Slack-Signature HMAC on every
# inbound event. Slack app settings → Basic Information → App Credentials.
# Use the ROTATED secret (spec §15) — the originals leaked into a transcript.
SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=

# Slack bot user OAuth token, "xoxb-" prefixed. Used for chat.getPermalink and
# chat.postMessage. Slack app settings → OAuth & Permissions.
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=

# Zep Cloud API key (Zep dashboard -> Project -> API keys). Powers the org and
# per-customer recall graphs. A missing key breaks memory projection only —
# ingest to D1 is unaffected.
ZEP_API_KEY=

# Anthropic API key. Phase 07 uses Haiku 4.5 for triage (~$0.0003/message);
# Phase 10 adds Fable 5 for the main agent on the same key.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Private AI Gateway for the Phase 10 agent's Fable calls. NOT YET CREATED --
# see docs/superpowers/plans/phase-10-notes.md; creating the gateway and
# issuing its token is a deferred operator step.
#
# The URL must be the PROVIDER-NATIVE Anthropic endpoint, of the form
#   https://gateway.ai.cloudflare.com/v1/<account-id>/<gateway>/anthropic
# because cf-aig-authorization is the auth header for that endpoint family and
# not for the api.cloudflare.com REST API. agent/model.ts enforces the host.
#
# AI_GATEWAY_TOKEN is a Cloudflare API token with AI Gateway `Run`, sent as
# `cf-aig-authorization: Bearer <token>`. It is a secret and must never appear
# in wrangler.jsonc, docs, generated types, or a test snapshot.
#
# ABSENCE IS NOT A MODE. A deployed Worker missing these does not park quietly;
# it composes, fails, and reports `configuration_incomplete` on /api/health and
# beside every run snapshot, naming the setting (never the value). That is what
# plan lines 965-966 require, and it replaced a presence check that left a
# dashboard full of `live` runs which would never move.
#
# Local unit tests need neither value: they inject a mock model, and the pool
# sets AGENT_MODEL_DISABLED so nothing can enter the production composer.
AI_GATEWAY_ANTHROPIC_URL=
AI_GATEWAY_TOKEN=

# The explicit model opt-out (agent/ports.ts). Only `1` or `true` disables;
# anything else, including a typo, leaves model work enabled so a mistake fails
# loudly. Set it here if you want `wrangler dev` to park model work instead of
# failing on the missing Gateway above. NEVER set it on a deployed Worker — its
# absence in production is what makes a missing Gateway URL visible.
#
# It cannot widen anything: it can only make the agent do less. There is no
# value of it that permits a direct, unmetered Anthropic call — agent/model.ts
# still refuses every URL it refused before.
AGENT_MODEL_DISABLED=

# Better Stack SQL API connection (Phase 09 Task 11, betterstack.logs()).
# NOT a bearer token: the log query path is HTTP Basic auth against a
# ClickHouse-compatible endpoint. Create at telemetry.betterstack.com ->
# Integrations -> SQL API -> Create connection. The password is shown once.
#
# The connection is REGION-SCOPED. Ours is eu-central-1a and can only see
# sources in that region; a source in another region resolves as
# NAMED_COLLECTION_DOESNT_EXIST, not as an empty result.
#
# Non-secret companions (endpoint host, source allowlist) live in
# wrangler.jsonc "vars" so the pinning is visible in a diff.
BETTERSTACK_SQL_USERNAME=
BETTERSTACK_SQL_PASSWORD=

# Better Stack Uptime API token (Phase 09 Task 11, betterstack.monitors()).
# A separate product and a separate credential from the SQL connection above:
# bearer auth against https://uptime.betterstack.com/api/v2. Create at
# uptime.betterstack.com -> Settings -> API tokens -> Team-based tokens.
#
# SECURITY: this token is read-WRITE (it can create, pause, and delete
# monitors) and GET /monitors returns monitor secrets in each record --
# auth_username, auth_password, request_headers, environment_variables,
# playwright_script. monitors() must project an explicit field ALLOWLIST;
# passing the API response through, or denylisting known-bad keys, leaks
# credentials for whatever endpoints the monitors authenticate against.
BETTERSTACK_UPTIME_TOKEN=

# Linear personal API key, "lin_api_" prefixed (Phase 09 Task 8). Linear ->
# Settings -> Security & access -> Personal API keys.
#
# Sent as a BARE Authorization header against https://api.linear.app/graphql --
# `Authorization: lin_api_...`, with NO "Bearer " prefix. Verified 2026-08-12.
#
# SECURITY: a personal API key inherits the full access of the human who
# minted it. Ours resolves all five workspace teams, including Development
# (the live team) and Marketing -- it is NOT restricted to the pinned team.
# The only thing keeping the agent out of real teams is the server-side pin
# in wrangler.jsonc vars (LINEAR_TEAM_ID). Never let a caller-supplied team
# id reach the GraphQL call.
LINEAR_API_KEY=

# LangSmith API key (Phase 09 Task 10). LangSmith -> Settings -> API keys.
# Sent as the `x-api-key` header (NOT Authorization) against
# https://api.smith.langchain.com. Verified 2026-08-12.
#
# ONE KEY, BOTH DIRECTIONS, TWO PROJECTS. This comment used to say "Firefighter
# only reads" and that is no longer true.
#
#   READ  -- the `langsmith` capability pulls a CUSTOMER's traces so the agent
#            can investigate their AI. Project `tweakleaf`, addressed by ID
#            (LANGSMITH_PROJECT_ID). Endpoint: POST /runs/query.
#   WRITE -- the agent emits its OWN run as a trace: a root `chain` span, one
#            `llm` per model step, one `tool` per run_code call. Project
#            `fire-fighter`, addressed by NAME (LANGSMITH_TRACE_PROJECT),
#            because ingest takes a `session_name` and creates the project on
#            first write. Endpoint: POST /runs/batch. Same `x-api-key`.
#
# Both projects are pinned in wrangler.jsonc vars because this credential is
# workspace-wide, so neither may ever come from a caller.
#
# THE ONE CONSTRAINT ON THIS KEY, measured 2026-08-17: a LangSmith key is scoped
# to exactly ONE workspace -- `GET /workspaces` returns a single entry -- and a
# project in any other workspace is INVISIBLE rather than an error. So both
# projects must live in the workspace this key belongs to
# (LANGSMITH_WORKSPACE_ID). Get that wrong and nothing raises: a read comes back
# with zero runs reporting success, and a write lands in a project nobody is
# watching. If you swap this key, re-check `GET /workspaces` against
# LANGSMITH_WORKSPACE_ID before assuming either half still works.
#
# The emitter is switched on by LANGSMITH_TRACING in wrangler.jsonc (opt-in;
# only "true"/"1" enables) and its content by LANGSMITH_TRACE_PAYLOADS
# ("none" | "redacted"). LANGSMITH_TRACING is also what the official `langsmith`
# npm package auto-reads; we do not install it -- the emitter is hand-rolled --
# so nothing else picks it up today.
#
# SECURITY: an "lsv2_pt_" key is a personal token and inherits the creator's
# workspace role. Ours is Admin: it can delete runs and projects, manage
# workspace secrets, and exec sandboxes. These two paths need runs:read and
# runs:write. Still fixable by minting a scoped key; until then the bound on
# what this credential is USED for lives in the capability layer and in
# tracer.ts, not in the token.
LANGSMITH_API_KEY=

# Supabase PostgREST key (Phase 09 Task 9). Supabase -> Project Settings ->
# API keys. Sent as BOTH `apikey:` and `Authorization: Bearer` headers against
# $SUPABASE_URL/rest/v1. SUPABASE_URL itself is in wrangler.jsonc vars.
#
# USE THE PUBLISHABLE KEY ("sb_publishable_"), NOT THE SECRET KEY.
# The secret key ("sb_secret_") bypasses Row Level Security entirely: it can
# read and write every table regardless of policy. The publishable key is
# subject to RLS, so a SELECT-only policy makes read-only a guarantee the
# DATABASE enforces -- not a promise the capability layer makes. This is the
# second of the two layers spec'd in the plan ("Supabase is read-only twice"),
# and the only vendor in this phase that offers a credential-level backstop.
#
# Consequence: with no RLS policy granting SELECT, reads return empty rather
# than erroring. An empty result here means "no policy", not "no rows".
#
# Direct Postgres (DATABASE_URL / DIRECT_URL via the pooler) is deliberately
# NOT used: it needs Hyperdrive or raw TCP sockets from the Worker, and grants
# full SQL where the plan wants a bounded, allowlisted read surface.
SUPABASE_KEY=

# --------------------------------------------------------------- Phase 12
#
# The five identity/OAuth secrets. All optional on `Env` (see src/index.ts) --
# every route that needs one 503s naming the VARIABLE rather than proceeding
# without it, so a checkout missing all five still boots and still serves the
# rest of the API.

# The one key that seals every stored user token at rest (AES-GCM-256) AND
# signs the OAuth `state` parameter (HMAC-SHA256). One secret, two uses --
# WebCrypto keeps them apart: src/identity/crypto.ts imports it with usages
# encrypt/decrypt, src/oauth/state.ts with sign/verify, both non-extractable,
# so neither key can do the other's job.
#
# Generate with:  openssl rand -base64 32
#
# It MUST decode to exactly 32 bytes; importIdentityKey rejects anything else
# with SealError("bad_key"). Rotating it makes every existing row in
# `identities` un-openable -- getDecryptedToken throws SealError rather than
# reporting "not connected" -- so a rotation means every fire-fighter
# re-connects both accounts. That is deliberate: silently treating a
# mis-rotated key as "nobody is connected" would hide the mistake.
IDENTITY_KEY=

# Slack OAuth v2 app credentials. Slack API -> your app -> Basic Information.
# The flow requests the USER scope `chat:write` (authed_user), not a bot
# scope: the point is posting AS the fire-fighter. The callback URL
# https://<origin>/api/oauth/slack/callback must be registered in the app's
# OAuth & Permissions -> Redirect URLs; the origin is derived from the
# request, so localhost and production need separate entries.
SLACK_CLIENT_ID=
SLACK_CLIENT_SECRET=

# GitHub OAuth app (a plain OAuth app, not a GitHub App -- see the phase 12
# plan's "What this phase deliberately does not do"). GitHub -> Settings ->
# Developer settings -> OAuth Apps. Scope requested: `repo`. Authorization
# callback URL: https://<origin>/api/oauth/github/callback.
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --------------------------------------------------------------- Phase 13
#
# The engineer nudge. These three are NON-SECRET and their real values live in
# wrangler.jsonc "vars" — a mode flag, a channel id and this Worker's own
# origin. They are listed here only so a local `wrangler dev` can override one
# without editing the committed config; leave them blank to use the pinned
# values. The nudge's credential is SLACK_BOT_TOKEN above, and it is spent in
# exactly two places: the nudge DM to the on-duty engineer and the chat.update
# that rewrites that DM once the card is settled. Never a customer message.
#
# NUDGE_MODE: "dm" (default) opens a direct message with the on-duty engineer;
# "channel" posts an @-mention into NUDGE_FALLBACK_CHANNEL_ID instead. Any
# other value reads as "dm".
NUDGE_MODE=

# NUDGE_FALLBACK_CHANNEL_ID: the #eng-firefighter channel id. Used in "channel"
# mode, and whenever the on-duty engineer has no Slack identity row -- fallback
# beats silence. Pinned EMPTY in wrangler.jsonc because the id is not known to
# this repo; with no value the nudge is DM-only and an unaddressable card stays
# on the sweeper's retry feed instead of being posted somewhere unintended.
NUDGE_FALLBACK_CHANNEL_ID=

# DASHBOARD_BASE_URL: the origin the nudge's "Review" button points at, as
# `${DASHBOARD_BASE_URL}/?approval=${id}`. Set it to http://localhost:8787 (or
# your dev origin) if you want a locally clickable button.
DASHBOARD_BASE_URL=

# --------------------------------------------------------------- Phase 18
#
# The Tier 2 sandbox's read-only monorepo credential. A fine-grained GitHub
# PAT: resource owner Zellify, ONLY `web2app-rebuild`, Contents: read-only.
# Create at GitHub -> Settings -> Developer settings -> Fine-grained tokens.
#
# It is spent in exactly ONE place -- the `outboundByHost` handler in
# src/sandbox/class.ts -- and only at EGRESS, after a request has already left
# the container. The container's git remote points at the sentinel host
# `git.firefighter.local` (wrangler.jsonc vars) carrying a placeholder string;
# the Worker rewrites the URL to github.com and swaps in this value on the way
# out. That is what makes "the container holds no write credential" true by
# construction rather than by discipline: the value never enters the container,
# so no `exec` inside it can read the value out of a file, a git config or
# /proc.
#
# SECURITY: read-only is not optional. This token is reachable by every run,
# and a run's whole purpose is executing model-authored code. A token that can
# write would make the container's isolation the only thing standing between a
# model and the monorepo's default branch. If the org blocks fine-grained
# tokens and a classic PAT has to be used, that widens the scope to every repo
# the creator can read -- record the fact rather than letting it pass quietly.
#
# The image build also needs it (as a BuildKit `--mount=type=secret`, so it
# never lands in a layer), but that is a build-time flag, not this file.
MONOREPO_PAT=

# The dev-tier environment the monorepo's Next.js apps validate with zod at
# startup, as ONE JSON object of string to string -- e.g.
# {"NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL":"...","SUPABASE_SECRET_API_KEY":"..."}. Sourced
# from Infisical's `dev` environment once and stored as a Worker secret,
# because the container has no Infisical auth.
#
# SECURITY: the model never sees, types, chooses or prints one of these values.
# Its code sets `injectDevEnv: true` and the Worker supplies the record to that
# one process; the values are never baked into the image and never set
# container-wide. A malformed blob fails loudly at parse time naming the key,
# never the value.
MONOREPO_DEV_ENV=

# Later phases add more. Add each here as its phase introduces it.
NUCLEO_LICENSE_KEY=
